.TH PPK 1 2026-11-02 "Peios ppk 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
ppk \- create and manage Peios packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ppk pack
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-depends
.I name constraint\c
.RB ]...
.RB [ \-\-requires
.I name constraint\c
.RB ]...
.RB [ \-\-provides
.I name version\c
.RB ]...
.RB [ \-\-claims
.I path target\c
.RB ]...
.RB [ \-\-description
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-homepage
.IR url ]
.RB [ \-\-license
.IR expression ]
.RB [ \-\-license\-class
.IR class ]
.RB [ \-\-source\-license
.IR expression ]
.I package path
.br
.B ppk fetch
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.B \-\-from
.I dir package
.br
.B ppk closure
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-closure\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-copy\-files ]
.IR package ...
.br
.B ppk compose\-env
.RI [ profile ]
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-symlink
.IR link ]...
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR path ]...
.RB [ \-\-include\-artifacts | \-\-copy\-artifacts ]
.RB [ \-\-copy\-files ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR package ...]
.B \-\-out
.I dir
.br
.B ppk run
.RI [ profile ]
.RB [ \-\-artifact\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-closure\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-copy\-files ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR package ...]
.RB [ \-\-
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B ppk help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B ppk
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ppk
creates Peios packages, stores them in an artifact directory, copies them
from one artifact directory to another, composes them into closures, and
lays closures out as the root of an environment or runs commands with them
at
.IR /usr .
.PP
A package is a manifest that lists every path in it, with each file's
content stored separately as a blob named by its SHA-256 hash.
Paths are relative to
.IR /usr :
a package's
.I bin/hello
is
.IR /usr/bin/hello .
The package's identity is the SHA-256 hash of its manifest, written as
.B sha256:
followed by the hash in lowercase hexadecimal.
.PP
A
.I package
argument is an identifier of the form
.IB name @ version\c
.RB [ ?arch= \c
.IR arch ].
.I name
is reverse-DNS: the domain of whoever owns the software, backwards, then
the product, as in
.BR dev.peios.hello ,
.BR org.gnu.gcc\-c++ ,
and
.BR io.github.eudev\-project.eudev .
It is at most 64 characters long and has at least three labels, separated by
dots.
Each label consists of lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.B +
and
.BR \- ,
starts with a letter or digit, ends with a letter, digit, or
.BR + ,
and does not contain
.BR \-\- .
The first label consists only of letters, and neither of the first two
contains a
.BR + .
.PP
.I version
is
.RI [ epoch\c
.BR : ]\c
.IB upstream \- revision\c
, as in
.BR 1.26.2\-3 ,
.BR 1.0\-rc.1\-1 ,
and
.BR 2:0.5.0\-1 .
.I epoch
is optional and
.I revision
is required; both are decimal numbers without leading zeros, and the
revision is at least 1.
.I upstream
is what follows the epoch, up to the last hyphen.
It consists of ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR ~ ,
and starts with a letter or digit.
Versions are ordered by epoch, then upstream version, then revision.
The upstream versions are compared a run of digits or a run of letters at a
time: digits as numbers, and letters by byte, except that
.BR dev ,
.B alpha
or
.BR a ,
.B beta
or
.BR b ,
.BR pre ,
and
.BR rc ,
in any case, mark a pre-release and sort in that order before the release.
Anything after a
.B ~
is a pre-release too, so
.B 1.0~rc1\-1
comes before
.BR 1.0\-1 .
This is the grammar and order of PSPU 5.5 and 5.6.
.PP
.I arch
consists of ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR ~ ,
and starts with a letter or digit.
Without
.BR ?arch= ,
the architecture is the one
.B ppk
was built for.
Use
.B noarch
for content that does not depend on the architecture.
No other qualifier is accepted, and
.B arch
can be given only once.
.SH PROFILES
An environment profile is a TOML file that says exactly what an environment
holds, for
.B compose\-env
and
.BR run :
.PP
.RS
.EX
dirs = ["proc", "sys", "tmp"]

[packages]
"net.busybox.busybox" = "1.37.0\-1"
"dev.peios.prelude" = "0.0.7\-1"

[symlinks]
"init" = ":sbin/prelude"
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B packages
maps each package's name to its exact version: a profile holds one version
of each package.
Every package is for the architecture
.B arch
gives, or the one
.B ppk
was built for.
.B symlinks
maps each link's path to its target, written as what follows
.IB path :
in
.BR \-\-symlink ,
so that a leading colon stands for
.IR usr .
.B dirs
lists empty directories.
.B include_artifacts = true
gives a composed root its packages as
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
does.
Every key is optional, and any other key is an error.
.PP
The packages named with
.B \-p
are added to the profile's, and the links and directories named with
.B \-\-symlink
and
.B \-\-dir
to its own.
Another version of a package the profile holds, or a link at a path the
profile links elsewhere, is an error.
.SH METADATA
Besides its files, a package's manifest can say what the package needs and
offers, and describe it.
.B ppk pack
records what its options give, and the manifest's identity covers it.
.PP
A dependency is on another package, by name.
A requirement is on a capability: a name that something provides, such as
the soname
.BR libc.so.6 ,
the command
.BR sh ,
or
.BR pkgconfig(zlib) .
A capability's name is 2 to 128 characters: ASCII letters of either case,
digits, and the characters
.BR \- ,
.BR . ,
.BR + ,
and
.BR _ ,
starting with a letter or digit and ending with one or with
.BR + ;
or a
.IB namespace ( argument )\c
, whose namespace is lowercase letters and digits starting with a letter, and
whose argument may also hold
.B :
and
.BR / .
.PP
Each dependency and requirement has a
.IR constraint :
one or more expressions separated by commas, all of which must hold.
An expression is one of
.BR = ,
.BR > ,
.BR >= ,
.BR < ,
.BR <= ,
and
.BR != ,
followed by a version, and a version alone means
.BR = .
The version may leave out its revision, and then the revision is not
compared:
.B >= 2.44
holds for
.BR 2.44\-3 .
An empty constraint holds for any version.
A provided capability has a version, which may also leave out its revision,
or none.
.PP
A claim is a link, at an absolute path, that the package provides besides
its files, which are all under
.IR /usr .
.PP
.B ppk
records and checks these, but nothing in
.B ppk
acts on them yet: no command chooses packages to satisfy dependencies, and
composing does not create claimed links.
.SH DERIVATION
.B ppk pack
also reads each ELF object among the files, and adds to the manifest what it
needs and provides.
An object's
.B DT_NEEDED
sonames become requirements, for any version, and its
.B DT_SONAME
becomes a capability the package provides.
A soname the package provides is not also required.
Files under
.IR src/ ,
which hold source rather than installed objects, are not read, nor are
symbolic links, which alias an object packed at its own path.
Other files that are not ELF are passed over.
.PP
For the glibc libraries
.RB ( ld\-linux\-x86\-64.so.2 ,
.BR libanl.so.1 ,
.BR libc.so.6 ,
.BR libdl.so.2 ,
.BR libm.so.6 ,
.BR libmvec.so.1 ,
.BR libnsl.so.1 ,
.BR libpthread.so.0 ,
.BR libresolv.so.2 ,
.BR librt.so.1
and
.BR libutil.so.1 )
and for
.BR libgcc_s.so.1 ,
whose symbol versions are numbered as their package is, the highest symbol
version that any object needs is a floor.
An object needing
.B GLIBC_2.34
of
.B libc.so.6
gives the requirement
.BR "libc.so.6 >= 2.34" .
A package that provides one of these libraries provides it at its own
version, such as
.BR 2.44\-1 .
.PP
The versions of
.B libstdc++.so.6
are not numbered as gcc is, so each is an exact capability instead:
.BI elfver(libstdc++.so.6: version )\c
\&.
A package providing
.B libstdc++.so.6
provides each version it defines, and an object needing a version requires
it, unless the need is weak.
.PP
What derivation finds joins what the options give.
Where both name the same capability, the option's entry is kept, unless it
gives no version or holds for any version, when the derived one is.
There are two exceptions.
A requirement on one of the floor libraries for anything but any version is
refused, because its floor comes only from the payload.
A provide of one at a version other than the package's is refused.
Either refusal names the written entry and what the payload gives, and
nothing is written.
.PP
An object that cannot be read, or whose version tables are inconsistent, is
an error naming it.
So is a package providing
.B libstdc++.so.6
that needs a version of it that it does not define.
A shared library named
.BI lib name .so
with no soname gives a warning on standard error, and nothing can depend on
it.
.PP
The same files give the same requirements and provides, in the same
manifest.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI pack " package path"
Pack the directory
.I path
as
.I package
and print the package's identity on standard output.
.IP
Regular files, symbolic links, and empty directories are recorded.
Directories with contents are implied by them.
A symbolic link is recorded with its target exactly as it reads and is never followed.
Of a file's permissions, only whether any execute bit is set is recorded.
.IP
.B ppk pack
refuses a directory that holds anything else, such as a socket or a device;
a file with its set-user-ID or set-group-ID bit set;
or a path or link target that is not valid UTF-8.
It also refuses an artifact directory inside
.IR path .
.IP
The manifest also records the metadata the options give, as
.B METADATA
describes.
A broken rule in it is a usage error, and nothing is written.
It adds what the files' ELF objects need and provide, as
.B DERIVATION
describes.
.IP
If
.I package
is already packed with the same contents and metadata,
.B ppk pack
succeeds without changing anything.
If it is packed with different contents or metadata,
.B ppk pack
fails and writes nothing: a packed version is never replaced.
.TP
.BI fetch " package"
Copy
.I package
from the artifact directory named by
.B \-\-from
and print its identity on standard output.
Blobs already present are not copied.
.IP
Nothing read from the source is trusted.
The source's ref must hold exactly one identity and a newline.
The manifest must hash to that identity, be in exactly the form
.B ppk pack
writes, name
.IR package ,
and contain only relative paths with no empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component.
Every blob copied must hash to the name the manifest gives it.
If a check fails,
.B ppk fetch
stops without writing the manifest or the ref.
Blobs it copied before the failure are named by their content and are kept.
.IP
If
.I package
is already present with the same identity,
.B ppk fetch
copies only what is missing.
If it is present with a different identity,
.B ppk fetch
fails and writes nothing.
.IP
These checks prove that the package matches the source's ref.
They do not prove who published it, so fetch only from sources you trust.
.TP
.BI closure " package\fR...\fP"
Compose the packages, each already in the artifact directory, into a
closure: one directory tree, the contents of a
.IR /usr .
Print the closure's identity on standard output.
.IP
Every file in the tree is a hard link to its blob, in the form its entry
needs, or a copy of it with
.BR \-\-copy\-files ;
symbolic links and directories are created in the tree.
The closure's manifest lists the identities of its packages, sorted and
without duplicates, and the closure's identity is the SHA-256 hash of that
manifest, so the order and repetition of the arguments do not matter.
An existing closure is reused, however its files were placed.
A tree is built in a temporary directory and renamed into place when
complete.
.IP
.B ppk closure
fails, writing nothing, if two packages give one path different entries,
or if a path lies beneath a file or symbolic link of another package.
It fails, leaving nothing behind, if a hard link cannot be made because the
closure directory is not on the same mount as the artifact directory, or if
a blob it copies does not match its hash.
.TP
.BI compose\-env " \fR[\fPprofile\fR]\fP"
Lay out the root of the environment that
.I profile
and the options describe in the new directory named by
.BR \-\-out ,
for a tool such as an image builder to take, and print the identity of the
packages' closure on standard output.
At least one of
.I profile
and
.B \-p
must be given, and the environment must hold a package.
The root's
.I usr
is the closure of the packages, built in place as
.B closure
builds it: every file is a hard link to its blob, in the root's own
artifact directory if it has one and otherwise in the artifact directory
the packages are read from, or a copy of that blob with
.BR \-\-copy\-files .
Nothing is written to that artifact directory.
.IP
What lies outside
.I usr
belongs to the environment, not to any package, and comes from the
profile's
.B symlinks
and
.BR dirs ,
.BR \-\-symlink ,
.BR \-\-dir ,
.BR \-\-include\-artifacts ,
and
.BR \-\-copy\-artifacts .
Their paths are relative to the root, must not contain an empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component, and must not be
.I usr
or lie under it.
.IP
The root is built in a temporary directory beside it and renamed into
place when complete.
.B ppk compose\-env
fails, writing nothing, if the directory already exists, if the packages
clash as they would for
.BR closure ,
or if a path given lies under a symbolic link given.
It fails, leaving nothing behind, if a hard link cannot be made because the
directory is not on the same mount as the artifact directory, or if a blob
it copies does not match its hash.
To compose a root on another mount, give
.BR \-\-copy\-artifacts ,
or
.B \-\-copy\-files
if the root has no artifact directory of its own.
.TP
.BI run " \fR[\fPprofile\fR] [\fP" \-\- " command \fR[\fPargument\fR...]]\fP"
Run
.IR command ,
or
.I /usr/bin/sh
when no command is given,
in an environment: the caller's own view of the filesystem, in a mount
namespace of its own, with the closure of the packages of
.I profile
and
.B \-p
mounted at
.IR /usr .
At least one of them must be given.
Everything outside
.I /usr
is as the caller sees it, so a profile's
.B symlinks
and
.B dirs
do not apply.
The closure is built as
.B closure
builds it, or reused if it exists.
.IP
.B ppk run
then replaces itself with
.IR command ,
whose exit status becomes
.BR ppk 's.
.I command
is looked up through
.B PATH
inside the environment, unless it contains a slash.
The default shell is the closure's own, not the caller's, so it is only
there if one of the packages provides
.IR bin/sh .
The mount stays inside the environment: nothing outside it sees the
closure at
.IR /usr ,
and the command's children share the environment.
.IP
.I /usr
must already exist; an environment replaces it rather than creating it.
.TP
.BI help " \fR[\fPcommand\fR]\fP"
Print help for
.B ppk
or for one of its commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-package " \fIpackage\fR..."
For
.B compose\-env
and
.BR run ,
add every
.I package
that follows, up to the next option, to the environment.
Can be given more than once.
A profile must come before it, since what follows it is read as packages.
.TP
.BI \-\-artifact\-dir " dir"
Store packages in
.I dir
instead of
.IR /system/artifacts .
.B ppk
creates the directory if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-closure\-dir " dir"
For
.B closure
and
.BR run ,
keep closures in
.I dir
instead of the
.I closures
directory of the artifact directory.
.I dir
must be on the same mount as the artifact directory, unless
.B \-\-copy\-files
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-depends " name constraint"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package depends on the package
.I name
at versions that satisfy
.IR constraint ,
or at any version if
.I constraint
is empty.
.I name
and
.I constraint
are separate arguments.
Can be given more than once, for different packages.
.TP
.BI \-\-requires " name constraint"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package requires the capability
.I name
at versions that satisfy
.IR constraint ,
or at any version if it is empty.
Can be given more than once, for different capabilities.
.TP
.BI \-\-provides " name version"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package provides the capability
.I name
at
.IR version ,
or at no particular version if it is empty.
Can be given more than once, for different capabilities.
.TP
.BI \-\-claims " path target"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package claims the absolute
.I path
as a symbolic link to
.IR target ,
which is written into the link exactly as given.
.I path
has no empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component and does not end in
.BR / .
Can be given more than once, for different paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-description " text"
For
.BR pack ,
record a one-line description, in printable ASCII.
.TP
.BI \-\-homepage " url"
For
.BR pack ,
record the upstream project's
.B http
or
.B https
URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-license " expression"
For
.BR pack ,
record the package's license, conventionally an SPDX expression.
.TP
.BI \-\-license\-class " class"
For
.BR pack ,
record which kind of terms the license is:
.BR unknown ,
.BR free ,
.BR firmware ,
or
.BR proprietary .
.B unknown
is the default, and is recorded as nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-source\-license " expression"
For
.BR pack ,
record the license of the source the package was built from.
.TP
.B \-\-copy\-files
For
.BR closure ,
.BR compose\-env ,
and
.BR run ,
copy each file of the tree from its blob rather than hard link it, so that
the tree can be on any mount.
A hard link cannot cross a mount, even between two mounts of one filesystem,
as a sandbox's bind mounts can be.
Each copy is checked against its hash as it is made, and has the mode and
security descriptor of its blob, but as a file of its own: it takes its own
space, and a later change to the store's descriptors does not reach it.
A closure that already exists is reused as it is.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " dir"
For
.BR fetch ,
copy from the artifact directory
.IR dir .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " dir"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create the root at
.IR dir ,
which must not exist.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-symlink " link"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create a symbolic link, with
.I link
given as
.IB path : target\c
\&.
.I path
is where the link goes, and may not contain a colon.
.I target
is written into the link exactly as given, unless it starts with a second
colon, which stands for
.IR /usr :
.B init::sbin/prelude
links
.I init
to
.IR usr/sbin/prelude .
Such a target is relative, climbing back to the root from a link in a
subdirectory, so
.B a/b::bin/sh
links
.I a/b
to
.IR ../usr/bin/sh .
Missing parent directories are created.
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create the empty directory
.IR path .
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
For
.BR compose\-env ,
also give the root its own artifact directory,
.IR system/artifacts ,
holding the packages' refs, manifests, and blobs as hard links, so that
.B ppk
inside the environment finds them by default.
Only the packages composed are included, and not their closure.
The root must be on the same mount as the artifact directory.
.TP
.B \-\-copy\-artifacts
For
.BR compose\-env ,
give the root its own artifact directory as
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
does, but copy the packages into it, checking each as
.B fetch
does, so that the root can be on any mount.
The root's own files and its artifact directory then share nothing with
the artifact directory the packages are read from.
Copying is a way of including the packages, so this wins over
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
and a profile's
.BR include_artifacts .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program name and version and exit.
.SH SECURITY
.B ppk
makes no access checks of its own.
It needs write access to the artifact directory, and read access to the
directory being packed or the artifact directory being fetched from.
.PP
Every file
.B ppk
writes in the artifact directory gets a security descriptor whose
protected DACL grants Everyone read and execute, and SYSTEM and
Administrators full control.
Every directory it creates there, and the root of every closure tree, gets
the same grants, inherited by what is created inside it.
A file in a closure is a hard link, so it has its blob's descriptor.
Only the DACL is set: the owner stays whoever ran
.BR ppk .
Off Peios, where the system call does not exist, no descriptor is set.
.PP
.B run
needs no privilege on Peios: any process can create a mount namespace and
bind-mount into its own.
Reusing a closure only reads the store, so anyone can run a closure that
already exists; building one needs write access to the closure directory.
The closure is not mounted read-only, which needs a privilege; its files are
protected by their descriptors instead.
.PP
A package is readable by everyone on a machine it is stored on, so a
package must never contain a secret.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including printing help or the version.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as an unreadable file, an unwritable artifact directory, a
version that is already present with different contents, or a package that
is missing from the source or fails a check.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, a missing argument, a malformed
.IR package ,
or, for
.BR pack ,
metadata that breaks a rule.
.TP
.B 126
For
.BR run ,
the command was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
For
.BR run ,
the command was not found.
.PP
Once
.B run
has started its command, the exit status is the command's.
.SH FILES
Paths are relative to the artifact directory,
.I /system/artifacts
by default.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash
The content of one file that is not executable, uncompressed, named by the
SHA-256 hash of that content.
Mode 0444.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash .exec
The same for an executable file.
Mode 0555.
Content shipped both as executable and not is stored in both forms.
.TP
.IR manifests/sha256/ hash
A manifest, named by the SHA-256 hash of its bytes.
.TP
.IR refs/ name / version / arch
The identity of the package with that name, version, and architecture,
followed by a newline.
.TP
.IR closures/ hash
The tree of the closure with that identity, unless
.B \-\-closure\-dir
names another directory.
.TP
.IR closures/ hash .manifest
The manifest of that closure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ppk pack 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' hello \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Pack the directory
.I hello
into
.I artifacts
and print the package's identity.
The identifier is quoted because some shells treat
.B ?
as a wildcard.
.TP
.B ppk pack org.git.kernel.dash@0.5.13.5\-1 dash \-\-depends org.gnu.glibc '>= 2.44' \-\-provides sh '' \-\-claims /usr/bin/sh dash
Pack the directory
.I dash
as a package that depends on glibc 2.44 or later, provides the capability
.BR sh ,
and claims
.I /usr/bin/sh
as a link to
.IR dash .
.TP
.B ppk fetch 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' \-\-from shared \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Copy the package from the artifact directory
.I shared
into
.I artifacts
and print its identity, which is the one
.B ppk pack
printed.
.TP
.B ppk closure 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' 'dev.peios.greetings@2.0\-1?arch=noarch'
Compose the two packages into a closure in
.I /system/artifacts/closures
and print its identity.
.TP
.B ppk run \-p 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' \-\- /usr/bin/hello
Run
.I /usr/bin/hello
from the package, in an environment whose
.I /usr
is the package's closure.
.TP
.B ppk compose\-env initramfs.toml \-\-out initramfs
Lay out the root of an initramfs in
.IR initramfs ,
from the profile shown under
.BR PROFILES :
the two packages as its
.IR usr ,
.I init
linked to
.IR usr/sbin/prelude ,
and empty
.IR proc ,
.IR sys ,
and
.I tmp
